$177. In a morning stream. The number on the dashboard is larger than my weekly rent payment used to seem.

I close OBS. Close VoxMorph. Take off the wig. The face in the dead webcam preview returns   Eli, pale, tired, the lashes and brows looking orphaned without their frame. The person the 324 viewers don't know exists.

Marc is coming at 2. I have two hours.

I eat a microwave burrito. Rotate at one minute. But this time, while the microwave hums, I open the RegenaForm app on my phone. I don't select anything. I just... browse. The Tier 1 menu. The options I haven't purchased yet. Nose   500 $. Lips   400 $. Jawline   600 $. Cheekbones   400 $. The crop line, dropping lower with each purchase. The face reveal, getting closer with each modification.

I close the app. Eat the burrito. It tastes the same as every burrito   warm salt and texture   but today it tastes like fuel rather than defeat.

[[Marc's Visit|day_4_12_other]]


:: day_4_12_other

Marc arrives at 2:12 PM. Twelve minutes late. The Marcus standard.

Two quick knocks, a pause, one more. The rhythm he's been using since high school. I open the door.

Charcoal hoodie. Basketball shorts. January. A Tupperware of leftover curry in one hand, phone in the other. The scar on his left eyebrow catching the hallway light.

<<marc "Yo.">>

<<eli "Hey.">>

He walks in. Steps over the Monster can I still haven't picked up. The apartment welfare scan   mattress, desk, kitchen, closet door. The closet door is closed. The wig is inside. The RegenaForm bag is behind the mini-fridge. The monitors are off. Everything is where it needs to be, which is to say hidden.

He drops the curry on the counter. Turns. Looks at me. The Marcus scan   the direct brown eyes cataloging the state of things.

<<marc "You look rested.">>

<<eli "Sleeping better.">>

<<marc "Since when?">>

<<eli "Couple days.">>

<<marc "Huh. You do something different? New vitamins or whatever?">>

The lashes. The brows. The two modifications sitting on my face in plain sight. I feel my pulse spike.

<<eli "Just... sleeping more. Eating slightly better.">>

<<marc "Slightly better than burritos isn't a high bar.">>

<<eli "It's the bar I have.">>

He accepts it. The way Marcus accepts things   with a nod and a subject change, the gift of not pushing. He doesn't see the lashes because he's not looking for them. He doesn't see the brows because the change is incremental and his reference frame is "Eli always looks like shit" and today I look like slightly less shit and the delta isn't suspicious enough to investigate.

The curry goes on the hot plate. We eat standing at the counter   the same ritual, the same geography, two men in a studio apartment eating reheated chicken and rice.

<<marc "Devon's definitely getting fired.">>

<<eli "The sink guy?">>

<<marc "He dropped a customer's AirPods yesterday. Down the actual drain. The customer watched it happen. Watched his AirPods disappear into the plumbing.">>

<<eli "How do you drop AirPods down a drain?">>

<<marc "Devon finds a way. Devon could drop the sun if you gave him five minutes.">>

<<eli "At least it wasn't a phone this time.">>

<<marc "The bar for Devon keeps lowering and he keeps finding ways to go under it.">>

We move to the mattress. I grab the controllers   the good one for Marcus, the thrift-store replacement for me. The PS4 boots. Rift Walkers loads.

[[Play Rift Walkers|day_4_13_other]]

:: day_4_13_other
<<set $day_step to 13>>
We play. The rhythm is the same   the yelling, the panic, the call-and-response that strips the layers away.

<<eli "HEAL ME ">>

<<marc "I literally have no charges left. Dodge.">>

<<eli "Dodge WHAT? He's got ">>

<<marc "Six arms, yeah, I remember. Dodge the arms.">>

<<eli "That's not a strategy!">>

<<marc "Neither is dying!">>

Floor 16. Tied the record. The death is spectacular   a trap chain reaction into a boss attack that wipes us both in the same frame.

<<marc "Floor 16.">>

<<eli "Tied it.">>

<<marc "We break it next time.">>

We run it three more times. Floor 12. Floor 9. Floor 14. The game is meditation   for two hours, I'm not Ellie. I'm not ElliePlayz. I'm not a voice modulator or a wig or a dashboard. I'm Eli. Marcus's friend. A person on a mattress dying on Floor 12.

Marcus is the anchor. The fixed point. The person who doesn't know and doesn't need to know and whose not-knowing is the last clean surface in a life that's getting complicated.

Between runs, during loading screens:

<<marc "How's the streaming stuff going?">>

<<eli "Better. Trying some new approaches.">>

<<marc "New approaches like what?">>

<<eli "Content strategy. Timing. Presentation stuff.">>

<<marc "On EliPlays?">>

The half-second pause. The gap where the truth lives.

<<eli "Just... streaming in general.">>

<<marc "Cool. Whatever works.">>

He doesn't push. The gift.

<<marc "You seeing Kira today?">>

<<eli "She's picking me up at 6. Thai food.">>

<<marc "Thai food with Kira. You should make a move.">>

<<eli "Marc.">>

<<marc "I'm just saying. She clearly likes you. The heart emojis. The ramen. She drives you places.">>

<<eli "She's my friend.">>

<<marc "She's your friend who wants to be more than your friend and you're too busy being an idiot to notice.">>

<<eli "I'm not being an ">>

<<marc "You called her hot and then said 'kidding.' That's idiot behavior. Textbook.">>

<<eli "Can we play the game?">>

<<marc "I can play the game and tell you the truth simultaneously. Multitasking.">>

<<eli "Your multitasking sucks. You walked into a trap while lecturing me.">>

<<marc "The trap was a metaphor.">>

<<eli "The trap killed your character.">>

<<marc "My character died for a cause.">>

I throw a cheese puff at his head. He catches it in his mouth. Neither of us says anything.

At 4:30, Marcus stands. Stretches. Spine-crack.

<<marc "Gotta bounce. Pickup game at the gym.">>

<<eli "Have fun.">>

<<marc "Have fun with Kira. And Eli ">>

<<eli "Don't.">>

<<marc "Make. A. Move.">>

<<eli "Goodbye, Marcus.">>

<<marc "I'm just saying ">>

<<eli "GOODBYE.">>

He grins. Picks up his hoodie. Heads for the door.

<<marc "Whatever the new streaming approaches are   glad they're working. You seem different.">>

<<eli "Different how?">>

<<marc "Less like you're waiting for something to happen. More like something already did.">>

He leaves. The door closes. The apartment settles.

Something already did. He said it casually but the words have weight   the weight of observation from a person who's been watching me for two years. Something already did happen. Several somethings. A voice modulator. A wig. A clinic. $387 in four days. A face that's changing. A person that's splitting. Marcus can feel the shift without seeing the cause, the way you feel an earthquake before the shaking starts   a change in pressure, a vibration too low to hear.

4:45 PM. Kira at 6. An hour and fifteen minutes. I shower. Grey shirt. The uniform.

