<<kira "Hey!">>

<<eli "Hey.">>

She's wearing an olive jacket over a black turtleneck, the wire-frame glasses slightly crooked, one hoop earring catching the dashboard light. She looks good. She always looks good. I notice this the way I've always noticed it   distantly, appreciatively, the way you notice a sunset without expecting it to notice you back. She pulls into traffic.

<<kira "How was your day?">>

<<eli "Marc came over. We played Rift Walkers. Tied Floor 16.">>

<<kira "Is that good?">>

<<eli "It's our personal best. We've been stuck at 16 for months.">>

<<kira "Congratulations on your matching record of mediocrity.">>

<<eli "That's almost exactly what Marc said.">>

<<kira "Great minds.">>

She glances at me. The quick look   the friend-check, the Kira version of Marcus's welfare scan. Except this time the glance holds. A half-second longer than normal. She's looking at my face. Studying it the way she studies analytics   with the focused attention of a person whose brain doesn't accept data without processing it first.

<<kira "Eli.">>

<<eli "Yeah?">>

<<kira "Your face looks different.">>

The sentence lands like a dropped glass   sharp, sudden, the pieces scattering. I keep my expression neutral through sheer force of will and eight months of practice at hiding emotional responses from webcams.

<<eli "Different how?">>

<<kira "I don't know. Something around your eyes. They look... bigger? More open? And your eyebrows are ">>

She pauses. Tilts her head. The analytical gaze intensifying.

<<kira "Did you reshape your eyebrows?">>

The question is so specific, so precise, so exactly correct that my blood temperature drops three degrees. She's not guessing. She's observing. Kira Nomura   the girl who notices energy drink brand changes and remembers 3 AM voice frequencies   is looking at my brow arch and identifying the modification with the accuracy of a person who has looked at enough faces on enough screens to know what a natural brow looks like and what a sculpted one looks like.

<<eli "I, uh   I tried one of those eyebrow grooming kits? From the drugstore. YouTube tutorial.">>

<<kira "An eyebrow grooming kit.">>

<<eli "Yeah. For, like, shaping. It was a video about looking better on camera. For streaming.">>

<<kira "You shaped your eyebrows. For streaming.">>

<<eli "Is that weird?">>

She looks at me for two more seconds. I can see her brain working   the analytical engine processing the data, comparing the input (his eyebrows are visibly different) against the explanation (drugstore grooming kit + YouTube tutorial), testing the plausibility.

<<kira "...it's not weird. Lots of streamers groom. Male streamers too. It's just ">>

<<eli "Just what?">>

<<kira "You never cared about that stuff before. The skincare routine, the hair washing, now eyebrow shaping. It's like someone flipped a switch.">>

<<eli "Maybe someone did.">>

<<kira "What does that mean?">>

<<eli "It means I'm trying harder. Is that allowed?">>

She's quiet for a moment. The blinker clicks as she turns onto the main road. The sound is mechanical, rhythmic, indifferent to the conversation happening over it.

<<kira "Of course it's allowed. I'm glad you're trying. You look good, Eli. The eyebrows actually look really nice.">>

<<eli "Yeah?">>

<<kira "Yeah. They open up your eyes. Your eyes look bigger with the arch. It's a good shape for your face.">>

She says it analytically   the streamer in her assessing a visual change the way she'd assess a new overlay or a lighting setup. But underneath the analysis there's something else. Something that sounds almost like surprise, or recognition, or the specific feeling of watching a person you've been silently wishing would try harder actually start trying.

<<kira "Just... don't overdo it, okay? The 'you' version of better is good. You don't need to be someone else.">>

The sentence hits the center of my chest and detonates. You don't need to be someone else. Said by a person who doesn't know that I've been being someone else for 324 viewers and $177 and a wig in a closet. The irony is a blade so sharp I don't feel the cut until later.

<<eli "I won't overdo it.">>

<<kira "Promise?">>

<<eli "Promise.">>

She nods. Accepts it. Moves on. The drive continues through the January evening, the city transitioning from the grey daytime palette to the amber-lit nighttime version, streetlamps flickering on one by one like neurons firing in a waking brain.

<<kira "Oh   I forgot to tell you. I hit 13,000 followers today.">>

<<eli "That's amazing, Kira.">>

<<kira "It's weird, honestly. Like, I remember when hitting 500 felt like a miracle. Now 13,000 feels like math. Like it's just the number that comes after 12,900. The excitement scales down as the numbers scale up.">>

<<eli "Hedonic adaptation.">>

<<kira "Look at you using psychology terms.">>

<<eli "I read it on a Reddit thread about streamer burnout.">>

<<kira "The fact that you're reading about streamer burnout is either promising or concerning and I can't tell which.">>

<<eli "Both.">>

She laughs. The real one   the crinkle-nose, glasses-slip burst that she tries to suppress and fails. The laugh that I filed in the Kira Archive months ago under "favorite sounds." It fills the car the way the cherry blossom fills the air   pervasive, warm, belonging to this specific space and this specific person.

<<kira "At least you're self-aware about it.">>

[[Go Inside|day_4_15_other]]

:: day_4_15_other
<<set $day_step to 14>>
<<set $hint = "Check the Menu and Order Food">>
The Thai place is called Lotus Room. Small, warm, red and gold decor, twelve tables. The smell of lemongrass and chili and coconut milk. We sit near the window   not a corner booth, not the intimate geography of a date, just a table for two by the glass where the street is visible outside and the restaurant is visible to anyone walking past.
<<set $locationName to "Lotus Room">>
<img src="images/locations/lotus_room.jpeg" alt="Lotus Room Interior" class="scene-image">
<<set $locationImage to "images/locations/lotus_room.jpeg">>
Friends at dinner. That's what this is.

<<kira "I'm getting pad thai. You?">>

<<eli "Green curry.">>

<<kira "Spice level?">>

<<eli "Medium.">>

<<kira "Coward.">>

<<eli "I prefer 'tactically cautious.'">>

<<kira "You prefer wrong.">>

She orders. In her minimal Thai   two words and a point and a smile that probably earns us better service than fluency would. The food comes fast. The pad thai is a generous tangle of noodles and bean sprouts and crushed peanuts with a lime wedge perched on the rim. My green curry is a pool of emerald coconut milk, steam rising like a signal.

We eat. The conversation spirals. She tells me about the brand deal she's been negotiating   a skincare company that wants her to say "this product changed my skin" and she's been pushing back because her skin hasn't changed.

<<kira "I told them I'd say 'I've been using this product' which is technically true because they sent me samples. But I won't say it changed my skin because it didn't change my skin. It's moisturizer. It moisturized.">>

<<eli "The integrity of moisturizer advertising.">>

<<kira "Don't laugh! This is real! If I lie about a moisturizer, my audience loses trust. And trust is ">>

<<eli "The most valuable currency.">>

<<kira "Exactly.">>

Trust is the most valuable currency. She says it like a mantra, like a first principle, like the bedrock of everything she's built. 13,000 followers built on the foundation of a person who won't lie about moisturizer. And I'm sitting across from her with engineered eyelashes and sculpted eyebrows and a wig in a closet and a voice modulator on my desktop and a fake name on a streaming account that's earned more in four days than her integrity has earned her this week.

The green curry is good. Rich, coconut-heavy, the kind of warmth that starts in the mouth and spreads to the chest. I eat and I listen and I talk and I'm present   genuinely present, the real Eli, the version Kira says is better in person   and the evening is settling into the comfortable rhythm that makes Kira's company feel like coming home to a home I never had.

Then the door opens.

The restaurant door. The front entrance, fifteen feet from our table. It opens and cold January air pushes in for a moment and a person steps through and the door closes and the warmth returns and nothing has changed except that I can't look away.

She's tall. Taller than Kira. 5'8", maybe 5'9", and the heeled boots add two inches beyond that, putting her at a height that commands attention the way a tall building commands a skyline. She's wearing a fitted dark dress   black, simple, ending above the knee   with a leather jacket over it, the collar turned up, the look of a person who dressed for an evening rather than a Thursday. Her hair is long. Past the shoulders. Dark brown with a slight wave, the kind of wave that could be natural or could be a curling iron and looks expensive either way, the kind of hair that moves when she moves, that catches the restaurant's red-gold light and absorbs it and gives it back warmer.

Her face is   I don't have the vocabulary. Or rather, I have too much vocabulary now, three days of YouTube tutorials and RegenaForm catalogs having given me a lexicon for beauty that I never wanted and can't unlearn. Her cheekbones are high and defined   "High & Defined" from the Tier 1 menu, the option that "creates shadows that photograph beautifully." Her lips are full with a pronounced upper-lip shape   "Cupid's Bow," the RegenaForm option that costs 450 $. Her eyes are large, dark, framed by lashes that are either genetic miracles or the product of a clinic not unlike the one four blocks from my apartment. The whole composition   face, hair, body, posture   is a catalog of the specific elements I've been studying and optimizing and performing, assembled on a real person with the coherence that my Ellie avatar is still trying to achieve.

She walks to a table across the room. Alone. Waiting for someone. She sits, crosses her legs   the movement drawing a line that my eyes follow involuntarily, from the ankle up the calf to the knee to the thigh where the dress hem rides up an inch and the skin above it is smooth and bare   and she pulls out her phone and the light from the screen illuminates her face from below, blue-white on the cheekbones and the lip contour and the column of her throat.

I'm watching the way I watch a stream. With the analytical focus I've developed over three days of studying feminine presentation. But beneath the analysis   beneath the clinical observation of "those cheekbones, that lip shape, that lash density"   there's the other thing. The older thing. The thing that isn't analytical at all. The thing that makes my chest warm and my hands still and my brain stop producing useful thoughts.

She's beautiful. Not the way Kira is beautiful   specific, angular, the beauty of intelligence and wire frames and sharp bobs. This woman is beautiful the way a landscape is beautiful. Broad. Undeniable. The kind of beauty that doesn't require context or personality or a 45-minute couch conversation to appreciate. The kind that stops scrolls and fills thumbnails and earns $487 in four hours and the kind that I am, in the secret hours of my life, trying to build on my own face.

Three seconds. Maybe four. That's how long I look at her before 

<<kira "Eli?">>

<<eli "Hmm?">>

<<kira "I was talking about the brand deal. The response rate they're asking for.">>

<<eli "Right. Sorry. What response rate?">>

<<kira "I switched topics thirty seconds ago. I was asking about your stream schedule.">>

<<eli "Sorry. I just ">>

<<kira "You're staring at that woman.">>

The sentence arrives flat. Not angry   Kira doesn't do angry the way other people do angry. She does controlled. She does precise. She does the specific vocal compression of a person who is holding something at arm's length and examining it before deciding whether to let it in.

<<eli "I wasn't ">>

<<kira "You were. It's fine. She's pretty.">>

The words "she's pretty" come out the way a scientist names a species   accurately, neutrally, with the implicit acknowledgment that accuracy and neutrality aren't the same as indifference.

<<eli "I wasn't staring. I just ">>

<<kira "Eli. I have eyes. I saw where yours went.">>

I have eyes. The phrase carries more weight than its three words. I have eyes too. As in: I see what you see. As in: I see the kind of beauty that catches your attention and it's the kind I'm not. As in: you told me I was hot in my apartment and then you laughed and said "kidding" and now you're staring at a woman in a fitted dress and the differential between that stare and the laugh is an equation I can solve and the solution is that you were kidding, you were always kidding, and the heart emojis I send every day are letters to a mailbox that nobody checks.

She doesn't say any of this. She says:

<<kira "Can we get the check?">>

<<eli "We literally just got our food.">>

<<kira "I'm not that hungry.">>

Her plate is half-full. The pad thai she ordered with the enthusiasm of a person looking forward to noodles is sitting there, cooling, abandoned. She doesn't look at it. She doesn't look at me. She looks at her phone, opens the payment app, and flags the server with a raised hand that's steady and professional and completely devoid of the warmth that was in it ten minutes ago.

<<eli "Kira, come on. I wasn't ">>

<<kira "It's fine. I have an early stream tomorrow. I should probably get home.">>

The lie is obvious and they both know it. Her stream isn't until noon. She told me in the car. "Early stream" is code for "I want to leave" and I can see the code and she knows I can see the code and neither of us acknowledges the encryption.

She pays. Card tap. The server brings the receipt. Kira signs it with the quick, clean signature of a person who wants to be anywhere but here. She stands. Puts on the olive jacket. Doesn't look at me while she does it.

I glance   involuntarily, reflexively, the way a person scratches an itch   at the stranger's table as we pass it. The woman is still there. Still alone. Still beautiful in the way that has nothing to do with me and everything to do with the algorithm that lives in the back of every human skull, the one that says look at that before the conscious mind can intervene.

Kira sees me glance. Her jaw tightens. The small muscles along the hinge visible beneath the skin. She walks faster toward the door.

<<set $hint = "Go Back to Kira's Apartment">>
[[Go Back to Kira's Apartment|day_4_16_other]]

:: day_4_16_other

The car ride home is a study in silence as architecture.

Cherry blossom. Heater. The same warm enclosure. But the warmth is wrong   the temperature is fine but the emotional temperature has dropped to something approaching the January outside. Kira drives with both hands on the wheel, ten and two, eyes on the road, the posture of a person who is concentrating on driving because concentrating on driving means she doesn't have to concentrate on anything else.

I sit in the passenger seat and I look out the window and I count the streetlamps   one, two, three   because counting is something to do that isn't talking and isn't thinking and isn't feeling the specific kind of shame that comes from hurting someone without meaning to.

Four blocks. Five. Six. The city scrolls past, amber-lit, indifferent to the atmosphere inside the Civic.

At block seven, Kira speaks.

<<kira "Can I ask you something?">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

<<kira "When you said 'it's hot' in my apartment   the click-through rate thing   and then you laughed and said you were kidding.">>

The air in the car changes. Thickens. The cherry blossom scent intensifies the way scents do in small spaces when the oxygen gets heavier.

<<eli "...yeah?">>

<<kira "Were you kidding?">>

The question has been sitting in her chest for two days. I can hear it in the way she asks it   not casually, not spontaneously, but with the deliberate cadence of a person who has practiced this sentence the way she practiced streaming, the way she practices everything, in the shower, alone, refining the words until they're precise enough to say out loud.

<<eli "Kira...">>

<<kira "Because from where I'm sitting   and I'm sitting right here, Eli, in this car that I drive to pick you up because I want to, not because I have to   from where I'm sitting, you said I was hot and then you took it back. And now tonight you're staring at a stranger who's hot and you're NOT taking it back. And I'm trying to figure out what I'm supposed to do with that difference.">>

The words land like stones in water. Each one accurate. Each one earned. The argument is constructed with the same analytical precision she brings to stream strategy and brand deals and the parasocial sweet spot   logical, evidence-based, airtight.

<<eli "I wasn't ">>

<<kira "You were. You looked at her the way people look at my stream thumbnail. The scroll-stopping look. I know what it looks like, Eli. I see it in my analytics every day.">>

She's comparing my stare to a click-through rate. She's quantifying my attention the way she quantifies viewer engagement. The streaming vocabulary has invaded her emotional life and it's devastatingly effective because the metaphor is perfect   I looked at the stranger the way the algorithm wants people to look at faces. The scroll-stopping look. And I didn't give it to Kira.

<<eli "That's not fair.">>

<<kira "What's not fair is sending someone heart emojis every night and then not sending them back. What's not fair is driving thirty minutes in January to pick someone up for dinner and watching them stare at someone else over the pad thai. What's not fair is asking someone if they were kidding and knowing they're going to dodge the question because they've been dodging everything since Tuesday.">>

She says it all in one breath. One long, measured, perfectly constructed breath that doesn't break and doesn't waver and doesn't crack. The emotional equivalent of her stream voice   controlled, professional, the performance of composure over a substrate of something raw.

Then she stops. Breathes. Her hands tighten on the wheel.

<<kira "I'm sorry. That was a lot.">>

<<eli "No. You're right.">>

<<kira "I'm not trying to be right. I'm trying to understand.">>

<<eli "I know.">>

<<kira "So help me understand.">>

I look at her. In the dashboard light, her face is half-illuminated   the wire-frame glasses catching green from the instruments, the jaw set, the lips pressed together. She looks tired. Not the physical tired I see in my own mirror   the tired of a person who has been carrying a feeling for a year and a half and the weight is showing.

<<eli "I wasn't kidding.">>

The words come out before the defense mechanism can catch them. Four words. Bare. No laugh. No hedge. No "haha just playing."

Kira's hands shift on the wheel. A micro-adjustment. She doesn't look at me.

<<eli "I wasn't kidding and I'm scared and I don't know what to do about any of it.">>

<<kira "Scared of what?">>

<<eli "Of everything. Of you. Of being the guy who can't buy coffee trying to date the girl with 13,000 followers. Of ruining the only good friendship I have because I can't keep my mouth shut about click-through rates.">>

<<kira "Eli ">>

<<eli "I'm scared that if we try this   if we cross that line   and it doesn't work, I don't just lose a girlfriend. I lose the person who tells me to eat real food and drives me to Thai restaurants and sends heart emojis I don't deserve.">>

The car slows. She's pulling up to my building. The curb. The same curb. The building with the broken elevator and the ceiling crack and the wig in the closet.

She puts the car in park. Doesn't turn off the engine. The heater hums. The cherry blossom hangs in the air. She looks at the windshield. Not at me. At the glass.

<<kira "You're an idiot.">>

<<eli "I know.">>

<<kira "A genuine, certified, clinical idiot.">>

<<eli "I know.">>

<<kira "The heart emojis aren't because I feel sorry for you. They're because I like you. I've liked you since the first time we played together. I've been not-subtle about it for a year and a half.">>

<<eli "I know.">>

<<kira "You KNOW?">>

<<eli "Marcus told me.">>

<<kira "MARCUS? Marcus who's never met me? Marcus TOLD you?">>

<<eli "He's very perceptive.">>

<<kira "He's very   I'm going to murder him.">>

<<eli "He means well.">>

<<kira "Eli. Look at me.">>

I look at her. She's turned in the driver's seat, her body angled toward me, the seatbelt cutting across the olive jacket. Her eyes behind the glasses are bright   not tears, not yet, but the pre-tear brightness, the surface tension of emotion held just barely intact.

<<kira "I need you to figure this out. I can't wait forever. I'm not going to sit in this car sending heart emojis into a void while you stare at other women. That's not a thing I'm willing to do.">>

<<eli "I know.">>

<<kira "Stop saying 'I know.' Say something real.">>

<<eli "I like you, Kira. I've liked you for a long time and I was too scared to say it and I'm still too scared and I don't know how to not be scared.">>

She looks at me. Three seconds. The dashboard hums. The cherry blossom hangs. The January cold presses against the windows from outside, wanting in.

<<kira "That's a start.">>

<<eli "Yeah?">>

<<kira "A really bad, emotionally stunted, pathologically avoidant start. But a start.">>

<<eli "So... what now?">>

<<kira "Now you go inside. And you think about what you want. And you text me tomorrow. Not a thumbs-up. Not a 'night kira' with no emoji. An actual text. With actual words. About actual feelings. Can you do that?">>

<<eli "I can try.">>

<<kira "Try harder than you tried with the click-through rates.">>

<<eli "...fair.">>

She doesn't lean across the console. Doesn't kiss me. But she reaches out and her hand   small, warm, the pixel-heart tattoo at the wrist   squeezes mine on the center console. One squeeze. Firm. A punctuation mark that says this isn't over but it isn't resolved either and you have homework.

<<kira "Goodnight, Eli.">>

Not "disaster." Not the nickname. Just my name. The real one. Said with a weight that names carry when they're being used seriously.

<<eli "Goodnight, Kira.">>

I get out. Close the door. The Civic idles at the curb. She doesn't pull away immediately   she waits until I'm at the building door, until I've hip-checked it open and stepped into the hallway, and then I hear the engine rev gently and the tires roll and the car pulls away and the cold fills the space where the cherry blossom was.

I stand in the hallway. The fluorescent light flickers overhead. The elevator makes its submarine sound behind the closed doors. The stairs stretch upward to the second floor, to the apartment, to the mattress and the desk and the fridge and the crack and the wig in the closet.

She said figure it out. She said text her tomorrow with actual words about actual feelings. She gave me homework.

The problem is that the feelings I have are real and the life I'm living is fake and the gap between those two things is the same gap that lives between EliPlays and ElliePlayz   measurable, growing, and funded by a secret that gets more expensive to confess with every passing day.

I take the stairs. Open the apartment. Stand in the dark.

[[Go to the Bedroom|day_4_17_other]]

:: day_4_17_other

The wig is in the closet. The VoxMorph is on the desktop. The dashboard has $177 from this morning and the night stream hasn't happened yet and the nose costs $500 and Kira just told me she likes me and I told her I like her back and neither of us kissed because we're both too scared and too smart and too aware that the timing is wrong in a way that neither of us can fully articulate.

I close the door. The night has just begun.

10:30 PM. The apartment. The ritual.

The evening sits in my chest like swallowed glass   Kira's voice saying "I need you to figure this out" and "I can't wait forever" and "text me tomorrow with actual words about actual feelings." The homework. The assignment I have no idea how to complete because the actual feelings include things like "I'm pretending to be a woman on the internet" and "I went to a bioengineering clinic and had my face modified" and "I've earned more money in four days as a fake girl than you earn in a week as a real one" and none of those qualify as the kind of actual feelings she's looking for.

I push it down. The way I push everything down. The apartment is cold and the fridge hums and the closet is closed and inside it the wig waits on its form like a costume between acts.

Wig on. Four minutes now. The routine getting faster. VoxMorph calibrated. Webcam tilted up. Desk lamp angled.

I check the OBS preview. Ellie's eyes look back at me. Large, lash-framed, brow-arched. The eyes that earned $177 this morning. The eyes that 324 people called beautiful. The eyes that are, right now, the eyes of a person whose best friend just told him to figure out his feelings and whose response is to put on a wig and stream for strangers.

The stream doesn't care about Kira's homework. The stream wants Ellie. And Ellie   whatever Ellie is, whoever Ellie is   doesn't have relationship problems. Ellie has viewers and donations and a community that says "you're safe here" and means it in the uncomplicated way that only strangers can mean things, because strangers don't know enough to be hurt by the truth.

ElliePlayz   Live
